infection in birthing parent"}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. A CN VI disorder"}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. Vitamin D deficiency in birthing parent"}]}, {"title": "A full-term newborn infant exhibits asymmetrical movement of the left upper", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Regards another’s face", "Human eyes are bright and shiny, so newborns should"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "Newborn: Personal-Social"}, {"type": "p", "text": "track caregivers’ eyes"}]}, {"title": "A full-term newborn infant exhibits asymmetrical movement of the left upper", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Cry: communication tool for hunger, pain, attention,", "High-pitched cry can indicate neurologic disorder", "Example: “Cri-du-chat”"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "Newborn: Language"}, {"type": "p", "text": "cold"}, {"type": "p", "text": "© 2026 Advanced Practice Education Associates \b 375"}]}, {"title": "It is important to assess reflexes in a newborn because this assessment does", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "which of the following?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. Indicates the maturity of the nervous system"}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. Indicates normal muscle tone"}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. Rules out neurologic disorders"}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. Assesses the gestational age of the infant"}]}, {"title": "Tonic neck", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "QUESTION #3"}, {"type": "p", "text": "At birth, the neonate’s head is usually which of the following sizes?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. 1–2 cm smaller than waist circumference"}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. 1–2 cm larger than chest circumference"}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. The same as the chest circumference"}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. The same as the waist circumference"}]}, {"title": "Tonic neck", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Milia and acne • Café-au-lait spots", "Congenital dermal melanocytosis • Port wine stains", "Lanugo • Hemangiomas", "Acrocyanosis • Stork bites", "Congenital nevi"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "Newborn: Skin NOTES"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Newborns: Skin"}]}, {"title": "Lanugo Hemangioma", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Molding", "Caput succedaneum", "Cephalohematoma"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "Newborns: Head"}, {"type": "p", "text": "© 2026 Advanced Practice Education Associates \b 377"}]}, {"title": "Lanugo Hemangioma", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "NOTES"}, {"type": "p", "text": "QUESTION #4"}, {"type": "p", "text": "A 1-week-old male newborn does not have a red reflex in his right eye. Which"}, {"type": "p", "text": "of the following might this indicate?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. Pupillary insufficiency"}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. Strabismus"}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. Congenital cataracts"}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. Congenital glaucoma"}]}, {"title": "Control and Prevention", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Red reflex should be present bilaterally at birth.", "Absent red reflex", "Consider congenital cataracts", "Retinoblastoma", "Vision is about 20/200 to 20/400"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "Newborns: Visual Behavior"}]}, {"title": "Control and Prevention", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Purulent ocular discharge", "Gonococcus", "Chlamydia", "Herpes", "Chemical conjunctivitis", "Erythromycin ointment used to prevent ophthalmia"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "Newborn: Ocular Discharge"}, {"type": "p", "text": "neonatorum (ON) caused by gonorrhea"}]}, {"title": "In a normally developed term infant, which of the following fontanels is", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "normally open at birth?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. Anterior only"}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. Posterior only"}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. Anterior and posterior"}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. Anterior always; posterior"}, {"type": "p", "text": "sometimes"}, {"type": "p", "text": "378\b FNP/AGPCNP Review"}]}, {"title": "When assessing an infant, you notice that they do not have a startle reflex", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "(arms extend from body with sudden head drop). This may indicate that"}, {"type": "p", "text": "this child has:"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. hearing loss."}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. central nervous system damage."}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. a visual defect."}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. developmental delays."}]}, {"title": "When assessing an infant, you notice that they do not have a startle reflex", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Low-set ears", "Renal agenesis", "Chromosomal abnormalities", "Assess gross hearing by observing for startle", "Auditory brainstem response", "Hearing screening at birth", "Repeat if negative", "Significant bilateral hearing loss", "0.1–0.3% of newborns", "2–4% of NICU graduates", "AAP recommends: universal hearing screening by 1 month", "Goal is to detect hearing loss before age 3 months", "Interventions by 6 months", "Ages 2–12 months", "Gross motor", "Fine motor", "Visual behavior", "Personal-social", "Language", "Reflexes", "Fontanels"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "Newborns: Ears"}, {"type": "p", "text": "response"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Newborn: Ears"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Infant Exam: Growth and Development"}]}, {"title": "NOTES 2-Month-Old Eat, Sleep, Repeat", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "QUESTION #7"}, {"type": "p", "text": "An infant weighs 7 pounds at birth. What would you expect this infant to"}, {"type": "p", "text": "weigh by age 1 year?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. 16 pounds"}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. 19 pounds"}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. 21 pounds"}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. 24 pounds"}]}, {"title": "Growth in Infants", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Newborns lose 5–7% of their birth", "Infant weight changes rapidly!", "Return to birth weight by age 2 weeks", "Gains 1 ounce/day", "Doubles birth weight by 4–6 months", "Triples birth weight by 1 year", "Head circumference increases 0.5 inches", "Lengthens 1 inch/month", "Grasps rattle if placed in hand", "Able to lift head 45° and has some control when"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "weight in the first days."}, {"type": "p", "text": "per month"}, {"type": "p", "text": "2-Month-Old: Motor Skills"}, {"type": "p", "text": "torso held upright"}, {"type": "p", "text": "© MartinPrescott/E+/Getty Images"}]}, {"title": "Growth in Infants", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Infant should:", "Focus on face", "Smile responsively", "Seem happy to see caregiver", "Calm when spoken to"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "2-Month-Old: Personal-Social Development NOTES"}]}, {"title": "Growth in Infants", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Vocalizes sounds that reflect happiness or being upset", "Makes short cooing sounds"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "2-Month-Old: Language Development"}]}, {"title": "Growth in Infants", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "QUESTION #8"}, {"type": "p", "text": "A 6-week-old infant has an open posterior fontanel. What does the NP tell the"}, {"type": "p", "text": "parents?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. This fontanel will stay open until about age 9 to 18 months."}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. The posterior fontanel should close by 3 months."}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. The baby should be referred to Neurology."}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. The posterior fontanel usually closes by 8 weeks."}]}, {"title": "Growth in Infants", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Intense eye contact", "Vertical and circular tracking", "Interested in mobiles", "Disconjugate gaze", "Red reflex present bilaterally"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "2-Month-Old: Visual Behavior"}]}, {"title": "Growth in Infants", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Head up to 90°", "No head lag when pulled upright", "Sits with head control", "Follows with eyes past midline", "Grasps rattle"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "NOTES 4-Month-Old: Motor Skills"}]}, {"title": "Personal-Social Development", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Smiles spontaneously", "Chuckles", "Makes sounds to get caregiver attention"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "4-Month-Old: Language & Vision"}]}, {"title": "Vision", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Intense eye contact", "Vertical and circular tracking", "Interested in mobiles", "Grasps toward hanging objects", "Disconjugate gaze"]}]}, {"title": "Language", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Vocalizes", "Oohs and aahs", "Laughs", "Turns head toward sound of voice"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "QUESTION #9"}, {"type": "p", "text": "An infant just learned to push himself up on all four limbs. How old is the"}, {"type": "p", "text": "infant likely to be?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. 1 month"}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. 2 months"}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. 4 months"}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. 6 months"}, {"type": "p", "text": "382\b FNP/AGPCNP Review"}]}, {"title": "Language", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Lots of changes!", "First teeth emerge", "Introduction of solid foods: “Food", "Introduce a cup", "Can try water with meals", "Anterior fontanel remains open"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "6-Month-Old: Development NOTES"}, {"type": "p", "text": "before 1 is just for fun!”"}]}, {"title": "Language", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Bears weight on legs", "Lifts chest using arm support", "Rolls over", "Sits with support, maybe unsupported"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "6-Month-Old: Motor Skills"}]}, {"title": "Language", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Squeals", "Turns toward rattling sound", "Takes turn making/mimicking sounds", "Blows “raspberries”"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "© miniseries/iStock"}]}, {"title": "Personal-social development", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Works to obtain toy", "Smiles often and interacts with caregiver", "Knows familiar people", "Looks at self in mirror", "Parachute reflex appears.", "Infant extends arms, hands, and fingers when", "Never disappears", "Many reflexes have disappeared by 6 months.", "Moro, stepping, rooting, palmar grasp, Galant,"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "6-Month-Old: Reflexes"}, {"type": "p", "text": "suspended prone and lowered quickly toward"}, {"type": "p", "text": "table."}, {"type": "p", "text": "tonic neck"}, {"type": "p", "text": "© NeoPhoto/iStock"}, {"type": "p", "text": "© 2026 Advanced Practice Education Associates \b 383"}]}, {"title": "Personal-social development", "blocks": [{"type": "p", "text": "NOTES"}, {"type": "p", "text": "QUESTION #10"}, {"type": "p", "text": "At what age should eye alignment occur?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. Within the first 2 months of life"}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. By 3 months of age"}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. By 6 months of age"}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. By 12 months of age"}]}, {"title": "Personal-social development", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Conjugate gaze", "Watches own hands", "Reaches toward and grasps hanging objects", "Color vision develops by 5 months"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "6-Month-Old: Visual Behavior"}]}, {"title": "Vision Screening in Children", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Once a child develops conjugate gaze (older than", "Strabismus: deviation of one eye due to", "Amblyopia: loss of vision in one eye", "When the two eyes don’t focus on the same object, the brain ignores information from one"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "6 months), they should be screened for strabismus."}, {"type": "p", "text": "oculomotor weakness ➔ amblyopia"}, {"type": "p", "text": "of the eyes."}, {"type": "p", "text": "QUESTION #11"}, {"type": "p", "text": "Which of the following tests is used to identify strabismus?"}, {"type": "p", "text": "1. Red reflex"}, {"type": "p", "text": "2. Snellen chart"}, {"type": "p", "text": "3. Cover-uncover test"}, {"type": "p", "text": "4. Visual tracking"}]}, {"title": "Strabismus Screening in Children", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Cover–uncover test to assess for strabismus", "Start at 6 months through 3 years", "Growth is about as rapid as at 6 months", "Length increases: 0.5 inches per month", "Weight increases: 3–4 ounces per week", "Head circumference increases: 0.25 inches per month"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "9-Month-Old: Growth"}, {"type": "p", "text": "384\b FNP/AGPCNP Review"}]}, {"title": "Strabismus Screening in Children", "blocks": [{"type": "list", "items": ["Pulls to sit • Holds to stand", "No head lag • Looks for yarn", "Sits without support • Rakes raisin using fingers", "Crawls/scoots • Passes a cube"]}, {"type": "p", "text": "9-Month-Old: Motor Skills NOTES"}]}], "pearls": ["NOTES QUESTION #6 When assessing an infant, you notice that they do not have a startle reflex (arms extend from body with sudden head drop). This may indicate that this child has: 1. hearing loss. 2. central nervous system damage. 3. a visual defect. 4. developmental delays.", "6-Month-Old: Reflexes • Parachute reflex appears. $ Infant extends arms, hands, and fingers when suspended prone and lowered quickly toward table. $ Never disappears • Many reflexes have disappeared by 6 months. $ Moro, stepping, rooting, palmar grasp, Galant, tonic neck © NeoPhoto/iStock", "$ Never disappears • Many reflexes have disappeared by 6 months.", "• PE: $ Muscle atrophy or injury $ Passive and active ROM $ Previous injuries $ Previous concussion Physical Exam Considerations • Eyes $ Refer if visual acuity is worse than 20/40."], "meta": {"source_chars": 39910, "section_count": 45, "pearl_count": 4}}